e{ln}⁡(1-s),\\,\\frac{1}{1+s}⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components. \n\n- The domain of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+s)$ is $s\\in (-1,\\infty )$ .\n\n- The domain of $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-s)$ is $s\\in (-\\infty ,1).$\n\n- The domain of $\\frac{1}{1+s}$ is  $s\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (-1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/3a/3ac2933afc460e42ad5bffb8b01b9fc3ff9275ab3bcf2ac3ab710a8094b36637.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(s)$ is\n\n$\\,$ $s\\in (-1,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281514 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115356 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "553f7b9d-c387-59b0-a0d6-9624d012e414" :knowledge-point/title "Domains of Functions With Trigonometric Components"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "138b2176-ecc6-57f2-bc6d-f1652a33636a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278767} {:answer/id #uuid "2444e896-56b2-5fe6-9c12-ded17b4df5eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi n}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278916} {:answer/id #uuid "5fb99ab6-2b20-5f51-ac07-2107a5729fb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi n}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279469} {:answer/id #uuid "9484619f-2ead-592d-844c-a164a8fc5f1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi n}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279912} {:answer/id #uuid "d1436dab-1fe3-50c6-a9d0-651b17cd161f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi n}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280437}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "138b2176-ecc6-57f2-bc6d-f1652a33636a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278767} :answer-field/id #uuid "064586b1-f0a2-55e9-ab4f-1edb28541b5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280841}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "355588e1-74ab-5c1c-9491-56bf772d1b60" :question/math-academy-id "q-162645" :question/problem "The domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)={t}^{3}\\,i+\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,j+\\sqrt{2{t}^{2}+1}\\,k$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne .\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression? You may assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component ${t}^{3}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(t+\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ except for the values of $t$ for which\n\n$$\nt+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $\\sqrt{2{t}^{2}+1}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\\,t\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\pi n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281522 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115355 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a1499c-080d-5008-bcc3-492ef469d3dc" :knowledge-point/title "Domains of Functions With Exponential and Logarithmic Components"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "172da4c2-2750-5a8d-a86a-b97c9604fbad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\in (1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278799} {:answer/id #uuid "7684847d-5d27-5cac-b143-1ec0db86e152" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\in [0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279647} {:answer/id #uuid "9ea5e2b2-027e-5a73-b7b3-004b4999d8bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\in [0,2)\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279999} {:answer/id #uuid "9fec719b-cb40-5f1c-9f1f-fe5c3833e5f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\in (-\\infty ,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280014} {:answer/id #uuid "f1ac898a-8049-5e2b-8b24-531f56c455b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\in [0,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1ac898a-8049-5e2b-8b24-531f56c455b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s\\in [0,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280697} :answer-field/id #uuid "951bda09-c9a0-55cd-9b45-dcd0ac3017cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "86e56d87-8f7f-5aac-9c51-653244b0266e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162659" :question/problem "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $f(s)=⟨\\sqrt{s},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-s),\\,\\sqrt{3}⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $\\sqrt{s}$ is $s\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-s)$ is $s\\in (-\\infty ,2).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $\\sqrt{3}$ is $s\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/80/808abeaa59d7bcea3b24e741fde5296791de32b4170d60f05f2a5af203c20ba2.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(s)$ is $s\\in [0,2).$"}]}
